#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:1
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_alternate_object_database]]alternate object database"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:5
msgid ""
"Via the alternates mechanism, a <<def_repository,repository>> can inherit "
"part of its <<def_object_database,object database>> from another object "
"database, which is called an \"alternate\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:6
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_bare_repository]]bare repository"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:16
msgid ""
"A bare repository is normally an appropriately named "
"<<def_directory,directory>> with a `.git` suffix that does not have a "
"locally checked-out copy of any of the files under revision control. That "
"is, all of the Git administrative and control files that would normally be "
"present in the hidden `.git` sub-directory are directly present in the "
"`repository.git` directory instead, and no other files are present and "
"checked out. Usually publishers of public repositories make bare "
"repositories available."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:17
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_blob_object]]blob object"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:19
msgid "Untyped <<def_object,object>>, e.g. the contents of a file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_branch]]branch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:30
msgid ""
"A \"branch\" is a line of development.  The most recent "
"<<def_commit,commit>> on a branch is referred to as the tip of that branch.  "
"The tip of the branch is <<def_ref,referenced>> by a branch "
"<<def_head,head>>, which moves forward as additional development is done on "
"the branch.  A single Git <<def_repository,repository>> can track an "
"arbitrary number of branches, but your <<def_working_tree,working tree>> is "
"associated with just one of them (the \"current\" or \"checked out\" "
"branch), and <<def_HEAD,HEAD>> points to that branch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:31
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_cache]]cache"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:33
msgid "Obsolete for: <<def_index,index>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:34
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_chain]]chain"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:38
msgid ""
"A list of objects, where each <<def_object,object>> in the list contains a "
"reference to its successor (for example, the successor of a "
"<<def_commit,commit>> could be one of its <<def_parent,parents>>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_changeset]]changeset"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:43
msgid ""
"BitKeeper/cvsps speak for \"<<def_commit,commit>>\". Since Git does not "
"store changes, but states, it really does not make sense to use the term "
"\"changesets\" with Git."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:44
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_checkout]]checkout"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:51
msgid ""
"The action of updating all or part of the <<def_working_tree,working tree>> "
"with a <<def_tree_object,tree object>> or <<def_blob_object,blob>> from the "
"<<def_object_database,object database>>, and updating the "
"<<def_index,index>> and <<def_HEAD,HEAD>> if the whole working tree has been "
"pointed at a new <<def_branch,branch>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:52
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_cherry-picking]]cherry-picking"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:59
msgid ""
"In <<def_SCM,SCM>> jargon, \"cherry pick\" means to choose a subset of "
"changes out of a series of changes (typically commits) and record them as a "
"new series of changes on top of a different codebase. In Git, this is "
"performed by the \"git cherry-pick\" command to extract the change "
"introduced by an existing <<def_commit,commit>> and to record it based on "
"the tip of the current <<def_branch,branch>> as a new commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:60
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_clean]]clean"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:64
msgid ""
"A <<def_working_tree,working tree>> is clean, if it corresponds to the "
"<<def_revision,revision>> referenced by the current <<def_head,head>>. Also "
"see \"<<def_dirty,dirty>>\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:65
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_commit]]commit"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:72
msgid ""
"As a noun: A single point in the Git history; the entire history of a "
"project is represented as a set of interrelated commits.  The word "
"\"commit\" is often used by Git in the same places other revision control "
"systems use the words \"revision\" or \"version\".  Also used as a short "
"hand for <<def_commit_object,commit object>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:77
msgid ""
"As a verb: The action of storing a new snapshot of the project's state in "
"the Git history, by creating a new commit representing the current state of "
"the <<def_index,index>> and advancing <<def_HEAD,HEAD>> to point at the new "
"commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:78
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_commit_graph_general]]commit graph concept, representations and usage"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:85
msgid ""
"A synonym for the <<def_DAG,DAG>> structure formed by the commits in the "
"object database, <<def_ref,referenced>> by branch tips, using their "
"<<def_chain,chain>> of linked commits.  This structure is the definitive "
"commit graph. The graph can be represented in other ways, e.g. the "
"<<def_commit_graph_file,\"commit-graph\" file>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:86
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_commit_graph_file]]commit-graph file"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:92
msgid ""
"The \"commit-graph\" (normally hyphenated) file is a supplemental "
"representation of the <<def_commit_graph_general,commit graph>> which "
"accelerates commit graph walks. The \"commit-graph\" file is stored either "
"in the .git/objects/info directory or in the info directory of an alternate "
"object database."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:93
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_commit_object]]commit object"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:99
msgid ""
"An <<def_object,object>> which contains the information about a particular "
"<<def_revision,revision>>, such as <<def_parent,parents>>, committer, "
"author, date and the <<def_tree_object,tree object>> which corresponds to "
"the top <<def_directory,directory>> of the stored revision."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:100
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_commit-ish]]commit-ish (also committish)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:110
msgid ""
"A <<def_commit_object,commit object>> or an <<def_object,object>> that can "
"be recursively <<def_dereference,dereferenced>> to a commit object.  The "
"following are all commit-ishes: a commit object, a <<def_tag_object,tag "
"object>> that points to a commit object, a tag object that points to a tag "
"object that points to a commit object, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:111
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_core_git]]core Git"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:114
msgid ""
"Fundamental data structures and utilities of Git. Exposes only limited "
"source code management tools."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:115
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_DAG]]DAG"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:120
msgid ""
"Directed acyclic graph. The <<def_commit_object,commit objects>> form a "
"directed acyclic graph, because they have parents (directed), and the graph "
"of commit objects is acyclic (there is no <<def_chain,chain>> which begins "
"and ends with the same <<def_object,object>>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:121
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_dangling_object]]dangling object"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:126
msgid ""
"An <<def_unreachable_object,unreachable object>> which is not "
"<<def_reachable,reachable>> even from other unreachable objects; a dangling "
"object has no references to it from any reference or <<def_object,object>> "
"in the <<def_repository,repository>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:127
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_dereference]]dereference"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:132
msgid ""
"Referring to a <<def_symref,symbolic ref>>: the action of accessing the "
"<<def_ref,reference>> pointed at by a symbolic ref. Recursive dereferencing "
"involves repeating the aforementioned process on the resulting ref until a "
"non-symbolic reference is found."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:139
msgid ""
"Referring to a <<def_tag_object,tag object>>: the action of accessing the "
"<<def_object,object>> a tag points at. Tags are recursively dereferenced by "
"repeating the operation on the result object until the result has either a "
"specified <<def_object_type,object type>> (where applicable) or any "
"non-\"tag\" object type. A synonym for \"recursive dereference\" in the "
"context of tags is \"<<def_peel,peel>>\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:142
msgid ""
"Referring to a <<def_commit_object,commit object>>: the action of accessing "
"the commit's tree object. Commits cannot be dereferenced recursively."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:145
msgid ""
"Unless otherwise specified, \"dereferencing\" as it used in the context of "
"Git commands or protocols is implicitly recursive."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:146
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_detached_HEAD]]detached HEAD"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:155
msgid ""
"Normally the <<def_HEAD,HEAD>> stores the name of a <<def_branch,branch>>, "
"and commands that operate on the history HEAD represents operate on the "
"history leading to the tip of the branch the HEAD points at.  However, Git "
"also allows you to <<def_checkout,check out>> an arbitrary "
"<<def_commit,commit>> that isn't necessarily the tip of any particular "
"branch.  The HEAD in such a state is called \"detached\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:164
msgid ""
"Note that commands that operate on the history of the current branch "
"(e.g. `git commit` to build a new history on top of it) still work while the "
"HEAD is detached. They update the HEAD to point at the tip of the updated "
"history without affecting any branch.  Commands that update or inquire "
"information _about_ the current branch (e.g. `git branch --set-upstream-to` "
"that sets what remote-tracking branch the current branch integrates with) "
"obviously do not work, as there is no (real) current branch to ask about in "
"this state."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:165
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_directory]]directory"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:167
msgid "The list you get with \"ls\" :-)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:168
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_dirty]]dirty"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:172
msgid ""
"A <<def_working_tree,working tree>> is said to be \"dirty\" if it contains "
"modifications which have not been <<def_commit,committed>> to the current "
"<<def_branch,branch>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:173
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_evil_merge]]evil merge"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:176
msgid ""
"An evil merge is a <<def_merge,merge>> that introduces changes that do not "
"appear in any <<def_parent,parent>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:177
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_fast_forward]]fast-forward"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:186
msgid ""
"A fast-forward is a special type of <<def_merge,merge>> where you have a "
"<<def_revision,revision>> and you are \"merging\" another "
"<<def_branch,branch>>'s changes that happen to be a descendant of what you "
"have. In such a case, you do not make a new <<def_merge,merge>> "
"<<def_commit,commit>> but instead just update your branch to point at the "
"same revision as the branch you are merging. This will happen frequently on "
"a <<def_remote_tracking_branch,remote-tracking branch>> of a remote "
"<<def_repository,repository>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:187
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_fetch]]fetch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:193
msgid ""
"Fetching a <<def_branch,branch>> means to get the branch's "
"<<def_head_ref,head ref>> from a remote <<def_repository,repository>>, to "
"find out which objects are missing from the local "
"<<def_object_database,object database>>, and to get them, too.  See also "
"linkgit:git-fetch[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:194
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_file_system]]file system"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:198
msgid ""
"Linus Torvalds originally designed Git to be a user space file system, "
"i.e. the infrastructure to hold files and directories. That ensured the "
"efficiency and speed of Git."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:199
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_git_archive]]Git archive"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:201
msgid "Synonym for <<def_repository,repository>> (for arch people)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:202
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_gitfile]]gitfile"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:207
msgid ""
"A plain file `.git` at the root of a working tree that points at the "
"directory that is the real repository.  For proper use see "
"linkgit:git-worktree[1] or linkgit:git-submodule[1].  For syntax see "
"linkgit:gitrepository-layout[5]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:208
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_grafts]]grafts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:214
msgid ""
"Grafts enable two otherwise different lines of development to be joined "
"together by recording fake ancestry information for commits. This way you "
"can make Git pretend the set of <<def_parent,parents>> a "
"<<def_commit,commit>> has is different from what was recorded when the "
"commit was created. Configured via the `.git/info/grafts` file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:218
msgid ""
"Note that the grafts mechanism is outdated and can lead to problems "
"transferring objects between repositories; see linkgit:git-replace[1] for a "
"more flexible and robust system to do the same thing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:219
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_hash]]hash"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:221
msgid "In Git's context, synonym for <<def_object_name,object name>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:222
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_head]]head"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:227
msgid ""
"A <<def_ref,named reference>> to the <<def_commit,commit>> at the tip of a "
"<<def_branch,branch>>.  Heads are stored in a file in `$GIT_DIR/refs/heads/` "
"directory, except when using packed refs. (See linkgit:git-pack-refs[1].)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:228
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_HEAD]]HEAD"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:235
msgid ""
"The current <<def_branch,branch>>.  In more detail: Your <<def_working_tree, "
"working tree>> is normally derived from the state of the tree referred to by "
"HEAD.  HEAD is a reference to one of the <<def_head,heads>> in your "
"repository, except when using a <<def_detached_HEAD,detached HEAD>>, in "
"which case it directly references an arbitrary commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:236
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_head_ref]]head ref"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:238
msgid "A synonym for <<def_head,head>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:239
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_hook]]hook"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:248
msgid ""
"During the normal execution of several Git commands, call-outs are made to "
"optional scripts that allow a developer to add functionality or "
"checking. Typically, the hooks allow for a command to be pre-verified and "
"potentially aborted, and allow for a post-notification after the operation "
"is done. The hook scripts are found in the `$GIT_DIR/hooks/` directory, and "
"are enabled by simply removing the `.sample` suffix from the filename. In "
"earlier versions of Git you had to make them executable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:249
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_index]]index"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:255
msgid ""
"A collection of files with stat information, whose contents are stored as "
"objects. The index is a stored version of your <<def_working_tree,working "
"tree>>. Truth be told, it can also contain a second, and even a third "
"version of a working tree, which are used when <<def_merge,merging>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:256
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_index_entry]]index entry"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:261
msgid ""
"The information regarding a particular file, stored in the "
"<<def_index,index>>. An index entry can be unmerged, if a "
"<<def_merge,merge>> was started, but not yet finished (i.e. if the index "
"contains multiple versions of that file)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:262
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_master]]master"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:268
msgid ""
"The default development <<def_branch,branch>>. Whenever you create a Git "
"<<def_repository,repository>>, a branch named \"master\" is created, and "
"becomes the active branch. In most cases, this contains the local "
"development, though that is purely by convention and is not required."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:269
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_merge]]merge"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:282
msgid ""
"As a verb: To bring the contents of another <<def_branch,branch>> (possibly "
"from an external <<def_repository,repository>>) into the current branch.  In "
"the case where the merged-in branch is from a different repository, this is "
"done by first <<def_fetch,fetching>> the remote branch and then merging the "
"result into the current branch.  This combination of fetch and merge "
"operations is called a <<def_pull,pull>>.  Merging is performed by an "
"automatic process that identifies changes made since the branches diverged, "
"and then applies all those changes together.  In cases where changes "
"conflict, manual intervention may be required to complete the merge."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:289
msgid ""
"As a noun: unless it is a <<def_fast_forward,fast-forward>>, a successful "
"merge results in the creation of a new <<def_commit,commit>> representing "
"the result of the merge, and having as <<def_parent,parents>> the tips of "
"the merged <<def_branch,branches>>.  This commit is referred to as a \"merge "
"commit\", or sometimes just a \"merge\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:290
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_object]]object"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:294
msgid ""
"The unit of storage in Git. It is uniquely identified by the "
"<<def_SHA1,SHA-1>> of its contents. Consequently, an object cannot be "
"changed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:295
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_object_database]]object database"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:299
msgid ""
"Stores a set of \"objects\", and an individual <<def_object,object>> is "
"identified by its <<def_object_name,object name>>. The objects usually live "
"in `$GIT_DIR/objects/`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:300
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_object_identifier]]object identifier (oid)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:302
msgid "Synonym for <<def_object_name,object name>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:303
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_object_name]]object name"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:307
msgid ""
"The unique identifier of an <<def_object,object>>.  The object name is "
"usually represented by a 40 character hexadecimal string.  Also colloquially "
"called <<def_SHA1,SHA-1>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:308
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_object_type]]object type"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:313
msgid ""
"One of the identifiers \"<<def_commit_object,commit>>\", "
"\"<<def_tree_object,tree>>\", \"<<def_tag_object,tag>>\" or "
"\"<<def_blob_object,blob>>\" describing the type of an "
"<<def_object,object>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:314
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_octopus]]octopus"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:316
msgid "To <<def_merge,merge>> more than two <<def_branch,branches>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:317
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_orphan]]orphan"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:322
msgid ""
"The act of getting on a <<def_branch,branch>> that does not exist yet (i.e., "
"an <<def_unborn,unborn>> branch).  After such an operation, the commit first "
"created becomes a commit without a parent, starting a new history."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:323
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_origin]]origin"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:330
msgid ""
"The default upstream <<def_repository,repository>>. Most projects have at "
"least one upstream project which they track. By default 'origin' is used for "
"that purpose. New upstream updates will be fetched into "
"<<def_remote_tracking_branch,remote-tracking branches>> named "
"origin/name-of-upstream-branch, which you can see using `git branch -r`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:331
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_overlay]]overlay"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:339
msgid ""
"Only update and add files to the working directory, but don't delete them, "
"similar to how 'cp -R' would update the contents in the destination "
"directory.  This is the default mode in a <<def_checkout,checkout>> when "
"checking out files from the <<def_index,index>> or a "
"<<def_tree-ish,tree-ish>>.  In contrast, no-overlay mode also deletes "
"tracked files not present in the source, similar to 'rsync --delete'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:340
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_pack]]pack"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:343
msgid ""
"A set of objects which have been compressed into one file (to save space or "
"to transmit them efficiently)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:344
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_pack_index]]pack index"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:348
msgid ""
"The list of identifiers, and other information, of the objects in a "
"<<def_pack,pack>>, to assist in efficiently accessing the contents of a "
"pack."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:349
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_pathspec]]pathspec"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:351
msgid "Pattern used to limit paths in Git commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:359
msgid ""
"Pathspecs are used on the command line of \"git ls-files\", \"git ls-tree\", "
"\"git add\", \"git grep\", \"git diff\", \"git checkout\", and many other "
"commands to limit the scope of operations to some subset of the tree or "
"working tree.  See the documentation of each command for whether paths are "
"relative to the current directory or toplevel.  The pathspec syntax is as "
"follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:363
msgid "any path matches itself"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:366
msgid ""
"the pathspec up to the last slash represents a directory prefix.  The scope "
"of that pathspec is limited to that subtree."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:370
msgid ""
"the rest of the pathspec is a pattern for the remainder of the pathname.  "
"Paths relative to the directory prefix will be matched against that pattern "
"using fnmatch(3); in particular, '*' and '?' _can_ match directory "
"separators."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:376
msgid ""
"For example, Documentation/*.jpg will match all .jpg files in the "
"Documentation subtree, including Documentation/chapter_1/figure_1.jpg."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:386
msgid ""
"A pathspec that begins with a colon `:` has special meaning.  In the short "
"form, the leading colon `:` is followed by zero or more \"magic signature\" "
"letters (which optionally is terminated by another colon `:`), and the "
"remainder is the pattern to match against the path.  The \"magic signature\" "
"consists of ASCII symbols that are neither alphanumeric, glob, regex special "
"characters nor colon.  The optional colon that terminates the \"magic "
"signature\" can be omitted if the pattern begins with a character that does "
"not belong to \"magic signature\" symbol set and is not a colon."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:391
msgid ""
"In the long form, the leading colon `:` is followed by an open parenthesis "
"`(`, a comma-separated list of zero or more \"magic words\", and a close "
"parentheses `)`, and the remainder is the pattern to match against the path."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:394
msgid ""
"A pathspec with only a colon means \"there is no pathspec\". This form "
"should not be combined with other pathspec."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:396
#, no-wrap
msgid "top"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:400
msgid ""
"The magic word `top` (magic signature: `/`) makes the pattern match from the "
"root of the working tree, even when you are running the command from inside "
"a subdirectory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:401
#, no-wrap
msgid "literal"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:404
msgid ""
"Wildcards in the pattern such as `*` or `?` are treated as literal "
"characters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:405
#, no-wrap
msgid "icase"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:407
msgid "Case insensitive match."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:408
#, no-wrap
msgid "glob"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:415
msgid ""
"Git treats the pattern as a shell glob suitable for consumption by "
"fnmatch(3) with the FNM_PATHNAME flag: wildcards in the pattern will not "
"match a / in the pathname.  For example, \"Documentation/{asterisk}.html\" "
"matches \"Documentation/git.html\" but not \"Documentation/ppc/ppc.html\" or "
"\"tools/perf/Documentation/perf.html\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:418
msgid ""
"Two consecutive asterisks (\"`**`\") in patterns matched against full "
"pathname may have special meaning:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:424
msgid ""
"A leading \"`**`\" followed by a slash means match in all directories. For "
"example, \"`**/foo`\" matches file or directory \"`foo`\" anywhere, the same "
"as pattern \"`foo`\". \"`**/foo/bar`\" matches file or directory \"`bar`\" "
"anywhere that is directly under directory \"`foo`\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:428
msgid ""
"A trailing \"`/**`\" matches everything inside. For example, \"`abc/**`\" "
"matches all files inside directory \"abc\", relative to the location of the "
"`.gitignore` file, with infinite depth."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:432
msgid ""
"A slash followed by two consecutive asterisks then a slash matches zero or "
"more directories. For example, \"`a/**/b`\" matches \"`a/b`\", \"`a/x/b`\", "
"\"`a/x/y/b`\" and so on."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:434
msgid "Other consecutive asterisks are considered invalid."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:436
msgid "Glob magic is incompatible with literal magic."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:437
#, no-wrap
msgid "attr"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:443
msgid ""
"After `attr:` comes a space separated list of \"attribute requirements\", "
"all of which must be met in order for the path to be considered a match; "
"this is in addition to the usual non-magic pathspec pattern matching.  See "
"linkgit:gitattributes[5]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:446
msgid "Each of the attribute requirements for the path takes one of these forms:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:448
msgid "\"`ATTR`\" requires that the attribute `ATTR` be set."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:450
msgid "\"`-ATTR`\" requires that the attribute `ATTR` be unset."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:453
msgid ""
"\"`ATTR=VALUE`\" requires that the attribute `ATTR` be set to the string "
"`VALUE`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:456
msgid "\"`!ATTR`\" requires that the attribute `ATTR` be unspecified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:459
msgid ""
"Note that when matching against a tree object, attributes are still obtained "
"from working tree, not from the given tree object."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:460
#, no-wrap
msgid "exclude"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:466
msgid ""
"After a path matches any non-exclude pathspec, it will be run through all "
"exclude pathspecs (magic signature: `!` or its synonym `^`). If it matches, "
"the path is ignored.  When there is no non-exclude pathspec, the exclusion "
"is applied to the result set as if invoked without any pathspec."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:468
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_parent]]parent"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:472
msgid ""
"A <<def_commit_object,commit object>> contains a (possibly empty) list of "
"the logical predecessor(s) in the line of development, i.e. its parents."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:473
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_peel]]peel"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:476
msgid ""
"The action of recursively <<def_dereference,dereferencing>> a "
"<<def_tag_object,tag object>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:477
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_pickaxe]]pickaxe"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:483
msgid ""
"The term <<def_pickaxe,pickaxe>> refers to an option to the diffcore "
"routines that help select changes that add or delete a given text "
"string. With the `--pickaxe-all` option, it can be used to view the full "
"<<def_changeset,changeset>> that introduced or removed, say, a particular "
"line of text. See linkgit:git-diff[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:484
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_plumbing]]plumbing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:486
msgid "Cute name for <<def_core_git,core Git>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:487
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_porcelain]]porcelain"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:492
msgid ""
"Cute name for programs and program suites depending on <<def_core_git,core "
"Git>>, presenting a high level access to core Git. Porcelains expose more of "
"a <<def_SCM,SCM>> interface than the <<def_plumbing,plumbing>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:493
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_per_worktree_ref]]per-worktree ref"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:498
msgid ""
"Refs that are per-<<def_worktree,worktree>>, rather than global.  This is "
"presently only <<def_HEAD,HEAD>> and any refs that start with "
"`refs/bisect/`, but might later include other unusual refs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:499
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_pseudoref]]pseudoref"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:503
msgid ""
"A ref that has different semantics than normal refs. These refs can be read "
"via normal Git commands, but cannot be written to by commands like "
"linkgit:git-update-ref[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:505
msgid "The following pseudorefs are known to Git:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:509
msgid ""
"`FETCH_HEAD` is written by linkgit:git-fetch[1] or linkgit:git-pull[1]. It "
"may refer to multiple object IDs. Each object ID is annotated with metadata "
"indicating where it was fetched from and its fetch status."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:512
msgid ""
"`MERGE_HEAD` is written by linkgit:git-merge[1] when resolving merge "
"conflicts. It contains all commit IDs which are being merged."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:513
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_pull]]pull"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:516
msgid ""
"Pulling a <<def_branch,branch>> means to <<def_fetch,fetch>> it and "
"<<def_merge,merge>> it.  See also linkgit:git-pull[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:517
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_push]]push"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:528
msgid ""
"Pushing a <<def_branch,branch>> means to get the branch's "
"<<def_head_ref,head ref>> from a remote <<def_repository,repository>>, find "
"out if it is an ancestor to the branch's local head ref, and in that case, "
"putting all objects, which are <<def_reachable,reachable>> from the local "
"head ref, and which are missing from the remote repository, into the remote "
"<<def_object_database,object database>>, and updating the remote head "
"ref. If the remote <<def_head,head>> is not an ancestor to the local head, "
"the push fails."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:529
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_reachable]]reachable"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:538
msgid ""
"All of the ancestors of a given <<def_commit,commit>> are said to be "
"\"reachable\" from that commit. More generally, one <<def_object,object>> is "
"reachable from another if we can reach the one from the other by a "
"<<def_chain,chain>> that follows <<def_tag,tags>> to whatever they tag, "
"<<def_commit_object,commits>> to their parents or trees, and "
"<<def_tree_object,trees>> to the trees or <<def_blob_object,blobs>> that "
"they contain."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:539
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_reachability_bitmap]]reachability bitmaps"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:546
msgid ""
"Reachability bitmaps store information about the "
"<<def_reachable,reachability>> of a selected set of commits in a packfile, "
"or a multi-pack index (MIDX), to speed up object search.  The bitmaps are "
"stored in a \".bitmap\" file. A repository may have at most one bitmap file "
"in use. The bitmap file may belong to either one pack, or the repository's "
"multi-pack index (if it exists)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:547
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_rebase]]rebase"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:551
msgid ""
"To reapply a series of changes from a <<def_branch,branch>> to a different "
"base, and reset the <<def_head,head>> of that branch to the result."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:552
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_ref]]ref"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:559
msgid ""
"A name that points to an <<def_object_name,object name>> or another ref (the "
"latter is called a <<def_symref,symbolic ref>>).  For convenience, a ref can "
"sometimes be abbreviated when used as an argument to a Git command; see "
"linkgit:gitrevisions[7] for details.  Refs are stored in the "
"<<def_repository,repository>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:563
msgid ""
"The ref namespace is hierarchical.  Ref names must either start with `refs/` "
"or be located in the root of the hierarchy. For the latter, their name must "
"follow these rules:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:565
msgid "The name consists of only upper-case characters or underscores."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:567
msgid "The name ends with \"`_HEAD`\" or is equal to \"`HEAD`\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:571
msgid ""
"There are some irregular refs in the root of the hierarchy that do not match "
"these rules. The following list is exhaustive and shall not be extended in "
"the future:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:573
msgid "`AUTO_MERGE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:575
msgid "`BISECT_EXPECTED_REV`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:577
msgid "`NOTES_MERGE_PARTIAL`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:579
msgid "`NOTES_MERGE_REF`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:581
msgid "`MERGE_AUTOSTASH`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:585
msgid ""
"Different subhierarchies are used for different purposes. For example, the "
"`refs/heads/` hierarchy is used to represent local branches whereas the "
"`refs/tags/` hierarchy is used to represent local tags.."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:586
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_reflog]]reflog"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:591
msgid ""
"A reflog shows the local \"history\" of a ref.  In other words, it can tell "
"you what the 3rd last revision in _this_ repository was, and what was the "
"current state in _this_ repository, yesterday 9:14pm.  See "
"linkgit:git-reflog[1] for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:592
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_refspec]]refspec"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:597
msgid ""
"A \"refspec\" is used by <<def_fetch,fetch>> and <<def_push,push>> to "
"describe the mapping between remote <<def_ref,ref>> and local ref. See "
"linkgit:git-fetch[1] or linkgit:git-push[1] for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:598
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_remote]]remote repository"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:602
msgid ""
"A <<def_repository,repository>> which is used to track the same project but "
"resides somewhere else. To communicate with remotes, see <<def_fetch,fetch>> "
"or <<def_push,push>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:603
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_remote_tracking_branch]]remote-tracking branch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:611
msgid ""
"A <<def_ref,ref>> that is used to follow changes from another "
"<<def_repository,repository>>. It typically looks like "
"'refs/remotes/foo/bar' (indicating that it tracks a branch named 'bar' in a "
"remote named 'foo'), and matches the right-hand-side of a configured fetch "
"<<def_refspec,refspec>>. A remote-tracking branch should not contain direct "
"modifications or have local commits made to it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:612
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_repository]]repository"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:619
msgid ""
"A collection of <<def_ref,refs>> together with an "
"<<def_object_database,object database>> containing all objects which are "
"<<def_reachable,reachable>> from the refs, possibly accompanied by meta data "
"from one or more <<def_porcelain,porcelains>>. A repository can share an "
"object database with other repositories via "
"<<def_alternate_object_database,alternates mechanism>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:620
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_resolve]]resolve"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:623
msgid ""
"The action of fixing up manually what a failed automatic <<def_merge,merge>> "
"left behind."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:624
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_revision]]revision"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:626
msgid "Synonym for <<def_commit,commit>> (the noun)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:627
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_rewind]]rewind"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:630
msgid ""
"To throw away part of the development, i.e. to assign the <<def_head,head>> "
"to an earlier <<def_revision,revision>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:631
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_SCM]]SCM"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:633
msgid "Source code management (tool)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:634
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_SHA1]]SHA-1"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:637
msgid ""
"\"Secure Hash Algorithm 1\"; a cryptographic hash function.  In the context "
"of Git used as a synonym for <<def_object_name,object name>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:638
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_shallow_clone]]shallow clone"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:642
msgid ""
"Mostly a synonym to <<def_shallow_repository,shallow repository>> but the "
"phrase makes it more explicit that it was created by running `git clone "
"--depth=...` command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:643
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_shallow_repository]]shallow repository"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:653
msgid ""
"A shallow <<def_repository,repository>> has an incomplete history some of "
"whose <<def_commit,commits>> have <<def_parent,parents>> cauterized away (in "
"other words, Git is told to pretend that these commits do not have the "
"parents, even though they are recorded in the <<def_commit_object,commit "
"object>>). This is sometimes useful when you are interested only in the "
"recent history of a project even though the real history recorded in the "
"upstream is much larger. A shallow repository is created by giving the "
"`--depth` option to linkgit:git-clone[1], and its history can be later "
"deepened with linkgit:git-fetch[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:654
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_stash]]stash entry"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:657
msgid ""
"An <<def_object,object>> used to temporarily store the contents of a "
"<<def_dirty,dirty>> working directory and the index for future reuse."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:658
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_submodule]]submodule"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:662
msgid ""
"A <<def_repository,repository>> that holds the history of a separate project "
"inside another repository (the latter of which is called <<def_superproject, "
"superproject>>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:663
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_superproject]]superproject"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:668
msgid ""
"A <<def_repository,repository>> that references repositories of other "
"projects in its working tree as <<def_submodule,submodules>>.  The "
"superproject knows about the names of (but does not hold copies of) commit "
"objects of the contained submodules."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:669
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_symref]]symref"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:675
msgid ""
"Symbolic reference: instead of containing the <<def_SHA1,SHA-1>> id itself, "
"it is of the format 'ref: refs/some/thing' and when referenced, it "
"recursively <<def_dereference,dereferences>> to this reference.  "
"'<<def_HEAD,HEAD>>' is a prime example of a symref. Symbolic references are "
"manipulated with the linkgit:git-symbolic-ref[1] command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:676
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_tag]]tag"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:685
msgid ""
"A <<def_ref,ref>> under `refs/tags/` namespace that points to an object of "
"an arbitrary type (typically a tag points to either a <<def_tag_object,tag>> "
"or a <<def_commit_object,commit object>>).  In contrast to a "
"<<def_head,head>>, a tag is not updated by the `commit` command. A Git tag "
"has nothing to do with a Lisp tag (which would be called an "
"<<def_object_type,object type>> in Git's context). A tag is most typically "
"used to mark a particular point in the commit ancestry <<def_chain,chain>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:686
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_tag_object]]tag object"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:691
msgid ""
"An <<def_object,object>> containing a <<def_ref,ref>> pointing to another "
"object, which can contain a message just like a <<def_commit_object,commit "
"object>>. It can also contain a (PGP)  signature, in which case it is called "
"a \"signed tag object\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:692
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_topic_branch]]topic branch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:698
msgid ""
"A regular Git <<def_branch,branch>> that is used by a developer to identify "
"a conceptual line of development. Since branches are very easy and "
"inexpensive, it is often desirable to have several small branches that each "
"contain very well defined concepts or small incremental yet related changes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:699
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_trailer]]trailer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:703
msgid ""
"Key-value metadata.  Trailers are optionally found at the end of a commit "
"message.  Might be called \"footers\" or \"tags\" in other communities.  See "
"linkgit:git-interpret-trailers[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:704
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_tree]]tree"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:708
msgid ""
"Either a <<def_working_tree,working tree>>, or a <<def_tree_object,tree "
"object>> together with the dependent <<def_blob_object,blob>> and tree "
"objects (i.e. a stored representation of a working tree)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:709
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_tree_object]]tree object"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:713
msgid ""
"An <<def_object,object>> containing a list of file names and modes along "
"with refs to the associated blob and/or tree objects. A <<def_tree,tree>> is "
"equivalent to a <<def_directory,directory>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:714
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_tree-ish]]tree-ish (also treeish)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:727
msgid ""
"A <<def_tree_object,tree object>> or an <<def_object,object>> that can be "
"recursively <<def_dereference,dereferenced>> to a tree object.  "
"Dereferencing a <<def_commit_object,commit object>> yields the tree object "
"corresponding to the <<def_revision,revision>>'s top "
"<<def_directory,directory>>.  The following are all tree-ishes: a "
"<<def_commit-ish,commit-ish>>, a tree object, a <<def_tag_object,tag "
"object>> that points to a tree object, a tag object that points to a tag "
"object that points to a tree object, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:728
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_unborn]]unborn"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:738
msgid ""
"The <<def_HEAD,HEAD>> can point at a <<def_branch,branch>> that does not yet "
"exist and that does not have any commit on it yet, and such a branch is "
"called an unborn branch.  The most typical way users encounter an unborn "
"branch is by creating a repository anew without cloning from elsewhere.  The "
"HEAD would point at the 'main' (or 'master', depending on your "
"configuration) branch that is yet to be born.  Also some operations can get "
"you on an unborn branch with their <<def_orphan,orphan>> option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:740
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_unmerged_index]]unmerged index"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:743
msgid ""
"An <<def_index,index>> which contains unmerged <<def_index_entry,index "
"entries>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:744
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_unreachable_object]]unreachable object"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:747
msgid ""
"An <<def_object,object>> which is not <<def_reachable,reachable>> from a "
"<<def_branch,branch>>, <<def_tag,tag>>, or any other reference."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:748
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_upstream_branch]]upstream branch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:753
msgid ""
"The default <<def_branch,branch>> that is merged into the branch in question "
"(or the branch in question is rebased onto). It is configured via "
"branch.<name>.remote and branch.<name>.merge. If the upstream branch of 'A' "
"is 'origin/B' sometimes we say \"'A' is tracking 'origin/B'\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:754
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_working_tree]]working tree"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:758
msgid ""
"The tree of actual checked out files.  The working tree normally contains "
"the contents of the <<def_HEAD,HEAD>> commit's tree, plus any local changes "
"that you have made but not yet committed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:759
#, no-wrap
msgid "[[def_worktree]]worktree"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/glossary-content.txt:766
msgid ""
"A repository can have zero (i.e. bare repository) or one or more worktrees "
"attached to it. One \"worktree\" consists of a \"working tree\" and "
"repository metadata, most of which are shared among other worktrees of a "
"single repository, and some of which are maintained separately per worktree "
"(e.g. the index, HEAD and pseudorefs like MERGE_HEAD, per-worktree refs and "
"per-worktree configuration file)."
msgstr ""
